  <h1>The Forbidden Library</h1>
  
 </div>
 <div class="page-nav"><span class="page-info">Page 2 of 4</span><div class="page-links"><a href="/the-forbidden-library?page=1#content" class="page-prev">&#8592; Previous</a><a href="/the-forbidden-library?page=3#content" class="page-next">Next &#8594;</a></div></div>
 <div class="story-body" id="content">
  <p>His mother had told him this when he was eight. He had asked her if it was true. She had said, "I don't know." She had said it the way people say it when they mean "I don't want to know," and he had understood this, and he had filed the story away in the place where childhood fears live, the place where they become less frightening as they become more real.</p>
<p>···</p>
<p>He went to the building on a Tuesday.</p>
<p>He had no plan. He had a library card with a symbol on it and no key and a feeling that he could not name. He walked down Arbor Street past the houses with their Halloween decorations and their porch furniture pushed back against the winter and he found the iron fence and he looked at the building through the bars.</p>
<p>It was not what he expected. He had expected something decrepit, something that matched the town's lie about fire, something with burned rafters or missing windows or the particular sagging quality of a building that has been abandoned. What he saw instead was a building that looked almost untouched. The brick was clean. The boarded windows were neat. The green paint on the door was fresh enough that someone had maintained it recently. The building sat at the end of the street like a thing that had been waiting.</p>
<p>He stood there for a long time. He was aware, in a way he could not explain, that someone was watching him. Not a person — the street was empty, the houses behind him were quiet, the afternoon was gray and still — but something, some presence, some attention that was being paid to him from inside the building or behind the trees or from some direction that he couldn't locate.</p>
<p>He took the library card out of his pocket. He held it up to the bars. He didn't know what he expected to happen.</p>
<p>The symbol on the card — the circle with the line through it — caught the light, and for a moment he could have sworn it moved. Not physically. Not the way a thing moves when the wind moves it. It moved the way a word moves when you read it and for a moment you don't recognize it, when the letters rearrange themselves into something you didn't expect.</p>
<p>He put the card back in his pocket. He walked home.</p>
<p>···</p>
<p>That night, he dreamed of the library.</p>
<p>In the dream, he was inside, in a room he didn't recognize — long, narrow, lined with shelves that extended up past the ceiling and into a darkness that the room shouldn't have contained. The shelves were full of books, but the books were not like any books he had seen. They were bound in materials he couldn't identify. Leather, or something like leather, but darker, and with a texture that suggested skin or bark or the surface of something that had once been alive and was no longer.</p>
<p>One of the books was on a reading stand in the center of the room, open, pages turned to a passage he couldn't make out because the writing was not in any alphabet he knew. He approached the reading stand. He reached for the book. And then he woke up, in his mother's house, in his mother's bed, with the taste of old paper in his mouth and the feeling — which he had been feeling for weeks, which he had almost gotten used to — that he was waiting for something.</p>
<p>He went back to the library on Wednesday. On Thursday. On Friday.</p>
<p>Each time he stood at the fence and looked at the building and felt the same strange attention being paid to him. Each time the card seemed to warm in his pocket, as if it were responding to something, as if it recognized the building and the building recognized it.</p>
<p>On Saturday morning, he found the key.</p>
<p>···</p>
<p>It was in his mother's desk, in a drawer he had already searched twice, in an envelope that was addressed to no one and sealed with tape that had dried to the color of the paper. Inside the envelope was a single key — old, iron, heavy in his hand — and a note in his mother's handwriting.</p>
<p>The note said: <em>For the door in the wall. Don't open it unless you mean to.</em></p>
<p>He knew, in the way that he always knew things about his mother — in the way that children know things about their parents that their parents never told them — that this was not a metaphor. That the door in the wall was a specific thing. That the warning was specific.</p>
<p>He put the key in his pocket. He didn't go to the library.</p>
<p>He sat in the kitchen instead. He drank coffee. He looked at the note. He read it again and again, as if repetition might change its meaning, as if the words might rearrange themselves into something safer. <em>Don't open it unless you mean to.</em></p>
<p>He thought about his mother. He thought about the library card he had found in her desk, the one with the symbol, the one that was issued by a library that was closed, that officially didn't exist. He thought about the story she had told him when he was eight. He thought about Adelaide Crane, who had built a library to trap something, and who had died refusing to eat, and whose will had stipulated that the library stay open forever.</p>
<p>He thought about the town, which had closed the library in 1985 and told everyone it had burned down.</p>
<p>He thought about the thing that might happen if a town that had been keeping something contained for a hundred and thirty years suddenly decided — quietly, without a vote, without anyone knowing — to let it go.</p>
<p>He picked up the key. He went to the library.</p>
<p>···</p>
<p>The door was not the main door.</p>
<p>He knew this before he found it. The key was too small for the main entrance, too old for the lock that had been replaced in 2003. It was a key for something else, something that had been there before the building was finished, before the door was hung, before any of it was anything more than a plan on paper.</p>
<p>He walked around the building to the side that faced the old cemetery — or what was left of it, a patch of ground behind the fence where the gravestones had been removed but the earth still showed the depressions, the memory of graves. The brick on this side was darker, older, the mortar between the bricks crumbling in places. And there, at ground level, set into the foundation as if it had been part of the stonework from the beginning, was a door.</p>
<p>It was small — not much taller than two feet — a kind of hatch or a gate, the kind of door that might lead to a cellar or a coal room. The wood was black with age. The iron handle was the same pattern as the fence, the same spikes, the same decorative rust. And above the door, carved into the brick, was the symbol — the circle with the line, the same symbol on his mother's library card.</p>
<p>He knelt. He put the key in the lock. It fit.</p>
<p>He could have stopped there. He could have taken the key and left and gone back to the house and waited for whatever was going to happen to happen. He could have let the town deal with the consequences of its own deception — the closed library, the forgotten contract, the thing that Adelaide Crane had spent her life containing and that the town had decided, without ever saying so out loud, to release.</p>
<p>He turned the key.</p>
<p>···</p>
<p>The room beyond the door was not what he expected.</p>
<p>He had expected — he didn't know what he had expected. A crypt, maybe, or a vault, or a chamber lined with the things that Adelaide Crane had collected to keep the library's bargain. What he found was a reading room.</p>
<p>It was small, no larger than a closet, and it was empty except for a single wooden chair, a reading stand, and a book.</p>
<p>The book was open on the stand. The pages were thick, slightly rough, the kind of paper that used to be made from rags. The text was in English — or something close to English, a version of English that had been filtered through centuries of careful editing, of translation, of the kind of copying that happens when people are trying to preserve something they don't fully understand.</p>
<p>He read the first line:</p>
